    "body": "Split from #8457.\n\n## Problem\n\n`prepare_autosp_inputs()` takes a `seq_dim` argument, but only passes it to `mark_dynamic`. The AutoSP graph passes ignore it: `pass_shard_seq_dim` and `shard_tensor_node` always read the sequence length from `shape[1]` and slice dimension 1.\n\nSequence-first `[seq, batch]` inputs (`seq_dim=0`) are therefore mishandled:\n\n- If the batch dimension is static, compilation fails with `expected sequence dimension to be of type SymInt but found int`.\n- If the batch dimension is dynamic, AutoSP shards the batch instead of the sequence, and attention runs over the wrong tokens.\n\n## Fix\n\nDynamo copies only the `tag` attribute of an input tensor into graph metadata (`_extract_tensor_dict`), so `seq_dim` has to travel in the tag:\n\n- `prepare_autosp_inputs()` tags inputs as `(key, seq_dim)`.\n- `find_node_by_tag()` accepts both the tuple and a bare key. A bare key, as set manually in existing code and tests, keeps the previous default of dimension 1.\n- New `get_autosp_seq_dim()` reads the recorded dimension. `pass_shard_seq_dim` and `shard_tensor_node` use it to find the sequence length, and `shard_tensor_node` builds its slice for the tensor's full rank.\n- `label_id` is now bounds-checked against `seq_dim`, like the other prepared inputs, since it is also sharded along that dimension.\n- Docs: explain that `seq_dim` applies to every prepared tensor, and that `seq_dim=0` is for sequence-first inputs.\n\n## Tests\n\n`TestAutoSPSeqDim` (2 ranks, `sp_size=2`) runs a small causal SDPA model with the sequence on `seq_dim` ∈ {0, 1}. For each of three batches, the mean of the per-rank AutoSP losses must match a single-device run on the full sequence (`rtol=1e-4`). Causal attention makes the loss depend on which dimension is sharded, so slicing the batch instead of the sequence would not match.\n\n- `seq_dim=0` fails on master and passes with this PR.\n- `seq_dim=1` passes on both, which covers the default path with the new tuple tags.\n\nThe test compares forward losses only. AutoSP currently sums gradients over the SP group, while each rank's loss is a local mean, so gradients are `sp_size`× a full-sequence reference. That is part of the causal-loss aggregation follow-up from #8457, not this change.\n\nResults on 4× NVIDIA RTX 5090 D, torch 2.13.0, transformers 5.17.0:\n\n- `tests/unit/v1/compile/test_compile_autosp.py -k \"not TestAutoSPCompile\"`: 38 passed.\n- `TestAutoSPCompile` fails identically on unmodified master in this environment. The error is a Dynamo `InternalTorchDynamoError` in the Ulysses baseline's `torch.compile` forward, before AutoSP runs, so I excluded it.\n- `pre-commit` passes on the changed files.\n",
